From 9b9a7b4481bbfb27dbd5e1ccc1d8a61c01c4296d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 22:39:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoRenderers: average the offset of the frames from the
 vsyncs on one branch

The clock sync of the render manager takes at every refresh
fmod(renderPts - nextFramePts, frametime) and, every 31 samples, moves both
the choice of frames and the clock that the audio engine follows by their
mean. fmod takes the sign of what it divides, so one offset is sampled on
two branches a period apart:
- where a new frame comes every second or fifth refresh (25 fps at 50 Hz,
  29.97 at 59.94, 23.976 at 119.88), the refreshes without one sample it a
  period below the others whenever it is above zero;
- an offset near a whole period, where one that drifts with the system
  clock gets in time (the default, without "Sync playback to display"),
  falls on either side of it.
The plain mean of the two branches is no offset at all: frames are chosen
at refreshes it does not say, and the sound follows an offset that is not
there.

Each sample is now taken on the branch nearest a reference before the mean
(CVsyncPhase): the first one on its branch nearest zero, then the mean of
each window, brought back within a period of zero and kept from one window
to the next. Where every refresh brings a new frame and the offset is away
from a whole period, the means are Kodi's, bit for bit. No sample is taken
while the clock stands: the latency is not in renderPts then, and with an
audio offset that is not a whole number of periods the samples of a pause
moved the offset.

In a host model of the render manager's choice (16 starts of 120 s each),
the frames shown for another number of refreshes than the ratio went from
457 to 0 at 23.976 fps on 119.88 Hz and from 93 to 0 at 25 fps on 50 Hz;
with one new frame per refresh every frame is chosen as before.

That this offset goes round, and taking its samples on the branch nearest a
reference that is kept, is SamuriHL's finding (his CoreELEC tree, e7e410fe35).
